# tty1 is this board's HDMI output, and the product has no GUI.
#
# Without this file the console shows a login prompt: 90-systemd.preset says
# `enable getty@.service`, getty@.service declares DefaultInstance=tty1, and the
# packed root ends up with etc/systemd/system/getty.target.wants/getty@tty1.service.
#
# A PRESET AND NOT A DELETED SYMLINK. An unenabled unit is an absence, not a
# decision -- one `systemctl preset-all`, one added preset file or one upstream
# packaging change puts the prompt back. `50-` sorts before `90-systemd.preset`,
# preset rules are read in basename order across /etc, /run and /usr/lib, and
# the FIRST match wins, so this resolves the unit to disabled. Same shape and
# same reason as 50-mos-ssh.preset and 50-mos-nftables.preset.
#
# THE CONSOLE IS NOT LOST. `console=tty1` keeps HDMI on the kernel's console
# list, so an oops or panic still takes the screen (PLAN-088 section 2.2), and a
# disabled unit is still startable: `systemctl start getty@tty1` turns the
# display into a login terminal at runtime, with no second boot path and no
# rebuild. systemd's getty-generator does not re-add one behind this file --
# it skips virtual consoles by design.
#
# The template is disabled rather than the tty1 instance, because autovt@.service
# is a symlink to it: naming only the instance would leave logind free to spawn a
# getty on VT switch, which is the same prompt by another route.
disable getty@.service
